  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n\nThis solution applies a systematic optimization strategy to refine the parent solution (720 points) and find a higher-quality minimum overlap configuration. The core idea is to use a coordinate-wise optimization approach with careful tuning of hyperparameters and tight budget management. While similar to previous methods, this strategy uses more aggressive search parameters and adaptive mutation schedules to escape local optima found by the parent solution. The key bottleneck addressed is the inability to globally optimize the function due to the high dimensionality and non-convex nature of the problem. We maintain rigorous adherence to the objective function while introducing two major improvements: 1) dynamic resolution adaptation allowing transitions to higher resolutions if beneficial, and 2) priority scheduling of expensive optimization methods based on remaining budget. These ensure we can break potential plateaus in the search space that the parent solution might miss.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\n$$C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))\\,dx$$\n\nsubject to:\n$$0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1, \\quad \\int_0^2 h(x)\\,dx = 1$$\n\nFor discrete implementation with dx=2/n:\n$$C_5 = \\max_k \\left[\\left(\\frac{2}{n}\\right) \\cdot \\text{autocorrelation}(h,k) \\right]$$\n\nThe algorithm uses:\n- Discrete domain with n points uniformly spaced in [0,2]\n- Stepwise coordinate updates with bitmask selection\n- Stochastic gradient descent with adaptive learning rate\n- Non-gradient-based binary push refinement\n- Resolution-aware interpolation and feasibility restoration\n\nThe solution vector h is kept as a NumPy array of length n, values must satisfy:\n$$\\sum_i h[i] dx = 1$$\n$$0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\forall i$$\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\n1. Input handling:\n   - Validate/initialize n, initial_h_values if provided\n   - Set target_sum = n/2 (due to dx=2)\n   - Initialize time tracking\n\n2. Core optimization loop by budget:\n\n   a) Variable-magnitude coordinate descent:\n      - Use sparse coordinate selection (BitMaskSelection)\n      - For each selected coordinate i:\n          Apply perturbation: h[i] = h[i] + m*(2*rand()-1)\n          Clip value and adjust to target sum via projection\n          Evaluate C5 candidate\n          Accept candidate if improvement exceeds threshold\n\n   b) Adaptive projected gradient descent:\n      - Use SmoothedObjective from gradients (defined in SmoothedMax)\n      - Apply multiple stages with increasing step size (GradientDescentStages)\n      - Implement projection step to enforce sum constraint ([0,1] handling)\n\n   c) Binary Push refinement:\n      - Non-gradient method for escaping flat regions\n      - Perform targeted binary push on promising solutions\n\n   d) Resolution transitions:\n      - Convert to higher resolution if budget/time permits\n      - Use interpolation preserving original solution\n      - Reapply gradient descent on larger domain\n\n   e) Selection and storage:\n      - Keep track of best solution encountered\n      - Periodically evaluate against parent solution check\n\n   Help: All non-convex terms computed via np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')*dx\n   Helper functions: SmoothedMax for gradient estimation, ProjectStep for constraint maintenance\n\n4. Search choices:\n\n   Default configuration:\n   - Primary method: CoordinateDescent with intensity steps: [-0.05, -0.02, 0.01, 0.02, 0.05]\n   - Secondary method: GradientDescent with stages (sigmas: [0.1,0.3,0.7,1.0])\n   - Population-based refinement: BinaryPush (15 iterations)\n   - Resolution limit: Start at 720, transition to 1000 or 1440 if improvements\n   - Budget splits: 1/4,1/4,1/4 search, 1/4 fallback\n\n5. Compute budget:\n\n   Main computation: n=720 \u2192 ~200ms/core/descent step\n   Transitions: n=720\u21921000 \u2192 ~500ms/core\n   Gradient evaluation: O(n^2) operations\n\n   Scheduling (example):\n   - Initial exploration: 400ms\n   - Value descent: 600ms\n   - Alternate search: 400ms\n   - Fallback: 100ms\n   - Total: 1500ms for n=720 base case\n\n   Monitoring: TimeLeft() checks at each major operation\n\n6. Validation:\n\n   For all candidates:\n   - Check finite C5 value\n   - Verify sum = target_sum\n   - Ensure 0<=all<=1\n   - Verify against previous record\n\n   Final output:\n   (h_best, c5_best, n_used)\n   \n   Where:\n   \u2022 h_best: float array implementing the solution\n   \u2022 c5_best: minimum achieved overlap value\n   \u2022 n_used: number of points used\n\nImplementation placeholders (to be fleshed out in the code):\n\nHelper functions:\n- SmoothedMax (gradient estimate function)\n- ProjectStep (constraint projection)\n- BitMaskSelection (random coordinate selector)\n- resolve_to_next() (resolution transition)\n- compute_c5() (objective calculator)\n- gradient_estimator() (stateful derivative approx function)\n\nThe code structure should execute within budget_s by:\n\n1. Setting up timing and resource limits\n2. Initializing from parent if present\n3. Executing main adaptive search phases\n4. Fallback to parent if stuck\n5. Outputting tracked best solution",
